// The Java target. NO PORTABILITY CLAIM.
//
// Every name here is Java's own. `java./` on ints truncates and throws on zero;
// `java.+` wraps at the width of its type; `java.==` on two Strings compares
// references, which is why `String.equals` exists separately. Nothing is
// renamed so three hosts can agree.
//
// The surface is one file per Java package or class, merged by LoadTarget.
// The portable layer it replaced, targets/portable-java.oro, was deleted in
// portable-2026-09-29.
// THREE OF THESE NAMES ARE RESERVED BY THE LANGUAGE, not chosen here:
// `int` and `f64` are what integer and float LITERALS type as, and `bool` is
// what `if` and a loop guard demand. A target spells them however it likes on
// the right; the names on the left are the language's.
//
// `int` is Java's **long**, deliberately: Java's `int` wraps at 2^31, which is
// INSIDE the range our integer literals cover, so declaring it would be a
// silent miscompilation (arithmetic.md §4). The consequence is that every
// array index needs a cast, because Java array indices are `int`.
// How this target spells an array of something (tables.md §10).
// The JVM has NO UNSIGNED TYPES, so 0..255 does not fit its `byte`
// (-128..127) and correctly selects `short`. Nothing special-cases that: the
// target declares what it can hold and the range picks. This is the same fact
// CLAUDE.md records as "the JVM has no unsigned types", stated where it can be
// acted on.
repr -128..127 host "byte"
// AND THE SAME byte READ UNSIGNED (ADR 0083): a host type and a second
// abstraction function, σ_u, so 0..255 is a byte[] and not a short[] — half
// the memory, and a file's bytes are Files.readAllBytes' own array, uncopied.
repr 0..255 host "byte" read "(${x} & 0xFF)" write "(byte) ${x}"
repr -32768..32767 host "short"
repr -2^31..2^31-1 host "int"
// THE WORD (ADR 0026): `int` is realized as `long`, 64-bit two's complement
// by the JLS, so native arithmetic is exact on this interval.
repr -2^63..2^63-1 word
// AND U = [0, 2^64−1], in the same `long` read UNSIGNED (ADR 0083: a host type
// and its abstraction function σ_u). Measured in u64repr-2026-09-22: within
// 1.25× of a signed long on multiply-add, 60× ahead of BigInteger. Its
// operations, the unsigned readings of Long's, are in u64.oro.
repr 0..2^64-1 word
set u64 = host "long"

set Array(A) = host "${A}[]"

// A MAP IS ONE DECLARATION, not an entry per (K, V) pair. This file's
// java/util module says the alternative is "the same limitation squared" --
// one name per (container, K, V) -- and it had declared exactly one
// instantiation, Map<String,Long>, because that is all it could afford.
set Map(K, V) = host "java.util.Map<${K},${V}>"

// AND ITS TYPE ARGUMENTS MUST BE BOXED, because a JVM generic cannot be
// instantiated at a primitive. `map int int` is `Map<Long,Long>`, never
// `Map<long,long>`. Which types a host boxes and what it calls them is a host
// fact, so it is declared here rather than hardcoded in the emitter -- no
// other target declares any, and on those `boxed` is just the type.
repr ref(Int) host "Long"
repr ref(F64) host "Double"
repr ref(Bool) host "Boolean"

// HOW MANY ELEMENTS A TABLE CAN HAVE HERE, and this host can say something
// stronger than the language can. `arraylength` returns an `int` and `new
// T[n]` takes one, so a Java array holds at most 2^31-1 elements. Every other
// length on this target is an `int` too -- `Map.size()`, `String.length()`.
//
// Without it a length is bounded only by ADR 0012's window at 2^53-1, which
// is true everywhere and true here, but too wide to hold an index. This is
// the platform fact indextype-2026-08-25 hardcoded in Go, stated where a
// target author can act on it.
fact max_len (a ∈ Array(A)) len(a) <= (2^31-1)

set Int = host "long"
set F64 = host "double"
set Bool = host "boolean"
set String = host "String"
set Any = host "Object"

// A HOST ERROR IS A TARGET-WIDE FACT, not a package's. `Exception` rather than
// `IOException` because the only thing this language can do with one is hold
// it, test it and hand it back (sums.md would be needed to read it, and that
// needs an interface, which callbacks.md tier 3 refuses) -- so a narrower name
// would be a claim about which calls may produce one.
set error = host "Exception"
// The try/catch templates' second result: the caught Exception, or null when
// nothing was thrown. 1 + E with null the absent point, a NICHE (spec/errors.md
// §4.1), through which a fallible declaration's failure is read.
repr error niche host expr "%s == null"

set jint = host "int"
set char = host "char"
set long_array = host "long[]"
set double_array = host "double[]"
set bool_array = host "boolean[]"
set string_array = host "String[]"
set strbuf = host "StringBuilder"

// java.util, which is not java.lang and needs an import — but is as much a
// part of the platform as anything else. The generic parameters are baked in
// because our type language has no constructors: List<Long> and List<String>
// are two unrelated atoms, and every other instantiation is unreachable.
set list_long = host "java.util.List<Long>"
set list_string = host "java.util.List<String>"
set map_string_long = host "java.util.Map<String,Long>"

// No `narrow`: a Java array cannot be re-sliced.
// `Main.java` by NAME, not the source directory. cmd/build fills the second
// hole with the work DIRECTORY and runs the command inside it, which `go build`
// accepts and `javac` does not — it wants source files. This target could not
// build a program at all until 2026-08-26, and nothing noticed because every
// Java result so far came through cmd/gen plus javac by hand.
//
// -encoding UTF-8 is not optional: javac defaults to the platform charset
// (strings.md §5).
build "javac -encoding UTF-8 -d %s Main.java"
// THE STRUCTURAL SET IS ZERO. `if`, `let` and `loop` are the LANGUAGE's and are
// injected into every target; declaring one is an error. The backend implements
// them, which is where an implementation belongs — a construct promoted to the
// language works on every target and the compiler finds the form.
//
